[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
What was reported down are the Campus 2 default gateways (SVIs) for VLAN 5 and VLAN 6, which live on the “big switch” device sshswitch (interfaces br_v5 = 10.5.0.254 and br_v6 = 10.6.0.254). At the time of investigation both SVIs are up and forwarding: sshswitch shows the IPs bound and UP on br_v5/br_v6, and tcpdump on sshswitch’s br_v5/br_v6 captures ICMP echo requests from hosts and immediate echo replies from 10.5.0.254/10.6.0.254. Multiple clients on 10.5.0.x and 10.6.0.x can now ping their gateways with 0% loss, and their ARP tables point to the gateway MAC 32:b8:77:f9:39:7d (the sshswitch MAC). The access switch (switch4) has the trunk subinterfaces eth0.5/eth0.6 bridged to the access ports correctly, so L2 is intact end-to-end. Inter-VLAN reachability tests (e.g., 10.5/10.6 to 172.16.10.x) also succeed, proving L3 forwarding via the gateways.

Most likely, right after the “reload,” the VLAN subinterfaces/bridges on sshswitch had not yet attached (or ARP/neigh tables were cold), so the SVIs temporarily didn’t answer ARP/ICMP. Once the bridge members (bond0.5/eth0.5 and bond0.6/eth0.6) settled, service returned.

Root cause: transient SVI/bridge bring-up latency on sshswitch after a reload.

Corrective fix: ensure ordered boot of bond0 → VLAN subifs (bond0.{5,6}) → bridges (br_v5/br_v6) and the physical trunk (eth0.{5,6}); use systemd dependencies (After/Requires) or ifupdown “auto” stanzas. Consider enabling STP and add monitoring/alerting for SVI status at boot. === END ===